many children have been returning to  school over the last few weeks and there  are still some open questions  about just how safe that is and what  happens if infections rise  over the winter well to talk about that  we're joined by the chair of global  public health at edinburgh  university professor debbie schrider  thank you very much for being on the  program this morning  schools are back should we expect to see  infection rates rise as a result  well that really comes down to the  testing and tracing system and how  quickly  cases within schools can be identified  through having surveillance in place  through having rapid testing in place  and how quickly the tracing teams can  ensure that  any other children in contact um with  this child have you know go into  isolation are tested as well  so it's really in our hands we've seen  variable experience in the world  from denmark which handled school return  exceptionally well  has kids back you know very little bump  in terms of their numbers  compared to israel where numbers just  skyrocketed because the testing and  tracing wasn't in place  i wonder as well if you could explain a  bit of a science to us because it does  feel as if there are lots of conflicting  reports out there  conflicting studies about the role that  children have in  spreading kovid 19 is there a difference  between  primary school-aged children secondary  age children what's your understanding  yeah so if we get back just to what we  know so far children  can have the virus regardless of their  age  they have the same viral load as adults  they can transmit the virus but for  an unconfirmed reason it seems primary  age children  transmit less than older children  meaning secondary age so we have seen  for example clusters and teenagers  occurring  and but primary school kids seem to be  less affected and some of the hypotheses  are about the social interactions of  primary school kids who they're around  they're around for example they're more  likely to be asymptomatic  which means coughing and sneezing is  less likely so not infecting others  as well as that primary school kids are  shorter and so they're less likely to  kind of be exposing adults or others  around  them so there is a difference but  science has not confirmed yet  what of those hypotheses it could be gas  medicine that could just be down to the  height of somebody on whether they  are passing it on or not um the  government is encouraging us to  get back to the office lots of people  are starting to go back into work now  which of course  poses child care issues for lots of  working parents  is it safe for grandparents to start  picking up child care responsibilities  now  well i think the the key answer to that  depends on what's happening locally  because is it safe enough is really the  bar you're trying to pass  if you're on a scottish island where  there's no new cases for weeks or other  parts of southwest of the  um of england then probably you're  pretty okay picking up picking up your  children because the likelihood of your  child  having an infection at school is so low  um if you're in a place which is in a  local  lockdown or has local restrictions in  place or numbers are increasing  um then i would say to be more cautious  just for that time  until hopefully those numbers come down  and this is why the government really  needs to commit to maximum suppression  of this virus  because children don't exist in a bubble  um all of us interact every day with  vulnerable individuals with elderly  individuals  they're a part of our society and we  need to ensure that we suppress the  virus so all of us can live  together um through this crisis we'll be  talking to dominic raab  uh after you uh and you're talking about  the government strategy  what do you make of where we are  currently uh when it comes to track and  trace when it comes to what the  government's overall strategy is  when it comes to cover 19. well i think  on the  positive side what we're seeing across  europe and this is true whether it's  germany whether it's now in britain  denmark  also for example sweden is they're  moving towards a model of maximum  suppression that if you want to get  normality back if you want the economy  to recover you need to control the virus  very tightly  and that's now i guess come together  that actually control the virus  you get your better economic recovery  and there i think the uk is committing  the key element here is getting the  testing faster and this is why  i'm a little bit worried about the back  to office push alongside the back to  school push  so what we've seen in scotland over the  past few weeks is the testing system has  just been  you know really having to race to catch  up with the demand of all the children  coming home with coughs and colds and  fevers  if you add on top of that all the adults  going back into offices and having those  as well your testing system is really  under strain  so you have to get ready get your  testing system going so people get the  results fast enough and the tracing  teams can get going  that's the main way to suppress this  virus and not have to put in place  restrictions  when the testing and tracing starts to  break down that's when you need your  local restrictions  where you have restrictions on let's say  going to other people's homes or on pubs  in hospitality or on riskier settings  so i think that's where we are now i  think we're unlikely to have another  national like lockdown we've learned a  lot about this virus  but it's up to the testing and tracing  and all of each of us and our behaviors  to make sure that  you know we make sure the virus stays  suppressed and we can keep things  running as much as fully as normally as  possible  um another big question that some are  talking about is whether or not they  should be testing at airports it's  something that  labour is calling for and we're of  course seeing increased infection rates  on the continent places like france and  spain do you think the government should  be looking at testing in airports  yes definitely i think the uk government  as a whole have been behind the curve in  terms of border control  one of the latest to put in border  restrictions and when they have been put  in place  um you know not really being monitored  in terms of compliance with them and  also being quite a harsh measure for the  aviation industry and and creating  uncertainty  so i think yes we definitely need a  better approach the problem with just  testing at an airport is that of the  incubation period of the virus meaning  people could test negative and then  you know go into pubs and bars and  engage fully in their lives and then  pass on the virus because they've just  been incubating it  you know in the pre um and symptomatic  phase  and so i think what we need to be having  is probably testing at airports plus  testing maybe  five to eight days later and then at  that point if you have two negative  tests and you can get on with your life  um but i think testing is really the key  to a lot of these issues of escaping  lockdown escape and quarantine  all those restrictions if you want to  lift them you got to get your testing in  place  we were talking a bit earlier about um  different risk levels i guess so if  you're a grandparent you might be  thinking that you can have different  interactions based on the infection  rates in your local area  and i just wondered there's so many  figures around  what numbers should it be that we're all  looking at you know should we be looking  at  case numbers hospital admissions the r8  what exactly should we trust  well i just look at three for different  countries when i try to compare them i  look at three pieces of data the first  is cases per 100 000  but these again are confirmed cases so  if countries are not testing enough they  might have  lower numbers so you also need to look  at your testing positivity that's the  number of people testing positive over  the total number  of people tested and that'll give you an  indication isn't enough testing  occurring  for example now it's parts the united  states you're having positivity between  30 to 40 percent which is incredibly  high  or in the uk luckily it seems to be  generally under two to three percent  um and the third is actually how many  cases are tied to clusters so if i see a  spike but most of them are coming from a  factory outbreak that seems like it  could be circled  you could do extensive testing it hasn't  spread you know widely then i'm less  worried  if you're seeing numbers go up and  they're all over the country and you're  unable to connect them  that tells you you've got substantial  community spread and that's more  worrying  so i guess it's those three things it's  your cases it's your positivity and it's  how many are tied to clusters  okay thank you so much for uh talking  through uh the figure it's always good  to have you on the show thank you  thank you for having me

